WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
Hold up your hands.

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:06.520
They are mirror images, yet you can't
lay one exactly on top of the other.

3
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:09.320
Some molecules come in
two forms like this:

4
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.020
a left-handed and a right-handed version.

5
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:14.140
An enzyme can tell them apart.

6
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:18.880
It binds one form, while the mirror
image binds weakly or not at all.

7
00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.260
So why can an enzyme grab one
molecule but not its mirror image?

8
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.180
In this mission, you'll
see how an enzyme works:

9
00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:31.720
a protein catalyst with a small pocket,
the active site, where the substrate binds.

10
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.760
Then you'll see how binding gives the reaction
a path with a lower activation energy.

11
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.870
Your mission: explain why an enzyme binds
one molecule but not its mirror image.